  "poi_santa_cristina_name": "Church of Santa Cristina",
  "poi_santa_cristina_desc": "Commissioned by Pope Pius VII Chiaramonti, who was born in Cesena, and designed by architect Giuseppe Valadier, the church was built between 1816 and 1825 in the Neoclassical style. Its circular plan, inspired by the Pantheon, is one of the building’s most distinctive features. During the Second World War, the crypt was reinforced and made safe for use as an air-raid shelter with room for up to 200 people. Father Leo Bagnoli remembered it as a solid, dry space where civilians sought refuge during air-raid warnings and the bombings that struck Cesena in 1944.",
  "poi_rocca_name": "Malatestiana Fortress",
  "poi_rocca_desc": "Construction of the Rocca Nuova, or Malatestiana Fortress, began in 1380 under Galeotto Malatesta and continued under Malatesta Novello on Garampo hill. After losing its military function, the fortress became a prison and remained one until 1969. During the Nazi-Fascist occupation it also held anti-Fascist prisoners. In 1944 the local Resistance carried out two raids to free political prisoners. The nearby Sferisterio, in front of the fortress entrance, was the site where eight anti-Fascists were executed on 4 September 1944. During the 1940s, the fortress’s underground spaces were also used as a shelter. The separate public shelter for 800 people often associated with the Rocca was a tunnel excavated into the hill along Viale Mazzoni.",
  "poi_san_rocco_name": "Church of San Rocco",
  "poi_san_rocco_desc": "The parish of San Rocco in Cesena dates back to 1615. In the summer of 1944, Allied bombing aimed at disrupting the German retreat by attacking the Ponte Vecchio also struck the San Rocco district. The church opened its rooms to civilians seeking shelter, and its bell tower was adapted as an air-raid refuge. A makeshift blast shield was built by filling a wardrobe with sand and placing it in front of the door. The tower also carried a siren and a bell, which could warn residents when the electricity failed. The church was destroyed in 1944; the only surviving part of the original building is its nineteenth-century bell tower. The parish was later rebuilt across the River Savio, in the Campino area.",
  "poi_abbazia_monte_name": "Abbey of Santa Maria del Monte",
  "poi_abbazia_monte_desc": "The Benedictine Abbey of Santa Maria del Monte was founded at the beginning of the 11th century on the hill known as il Monte, south-east of Cesena. During the Second World War, the monks built an underground gallery accessible through a well and enlarged with chambers carved into the tuff. It sheltered about 700 displaced people fleeing air raids and roundups. Many books from the Malatestiana Library were also kept underground, while its most valuable manuscripts had been moved to the Modena area at the beginning of the conflict. The monk Odo Contestabile helped protect Jewish families and, in 1943, escorted two families towards the Swiss border; he was recognised as Righteous Among the Nations in 2019. Between 16 and 19 October 1944, repeated bombing struck the abbey: the church was gutted and part of the monastery collapsed. None of the displaced people sheltering in the complex were killed.",
  "poi_osservanza_name": "Church and Convent of the Osservanza",
  "poi_osservanza_desc": "The church of the Osservanza was built between 1459 and 1464 at the behest of Malatesta Novello and was associated with the Order of Friars Minor of the Observance. Its design was entrusted to master builder Maso della Val Lugano. During the Second World War, the complex also served as a place of civilian protection: two air-raid shelters were set up, one in the catacombs and one near the bell tower, with a combined capacity of about 200 people. The church did not escape the fighting around the Gothic Line: it was hit by Allied raids on 16 and 19 October 1944. Father Bosoni died during those raids and is remembered by a plaque in the catacombs.",
  "poi_palazzo_ridotto_name": "Palazzo del Ridotto",
  "poi_palazzo_ridotto_desc": "Palazzo del Ridotto faces Piazza Almerici and stands on an earlier medieval structure. Sources differ on its first building phase: Resistenza mAPPe describes a building from 1401–1403, enlarged between 1466 and 1472, while the regional catalogue dates the construction of the Conservatori palace to 1466–1472 on an earlier structure. Its name is linked to the meeting place of Cesena’s nobility in the 18th century. During the Second World War, an air-raid siren was installed on its tower as part of the city-wide warning system; church bells also provided a backup when the electricity failed. After the partisan attack of 18 January 1944 on the Fascist headquarters at Palazzo Fantaguzzi, the local offices of the Republican Fascist Party moved here. The palace was also used to detain and torture prisoners. A plaque placed in 1995 on the side facing Piazza Almerici commemorates the Jews of Cesena murdered in the Holocaust.",
  "poi_stazione_name": "Cesena Railway Station",
  "poi_stazione_desc": "Cesena’s first railway station opened in 1861. A plan for a larger station building was approved in 1919, and its foundation stone was laid in 1925. The Arrigoni food factory stood beside the railway yard, while the Montecatini refinery, which processed sulphur from the Perticara and Formignano mines, stood on the opposite side. This concentration of transport and industrial facilities made the area a strategic target. The first major air raid on Cesena, on 13 May 1944, also hit the station; on 29 June another raid destroyed ammunition loads and set the refinery on fire. Further attacks followed in July, August and early September. During the Resistance, the station and railway lines were also targeted by partisan actions intended to disrupt supplies to the occupying army.",
  "poi_arrigoni_name": "Arrigoni Factory",
  "poi_arrigoni_desc": "The Arrigoni factory stood next to Cesena railway station. It became one of the area’s leading food factories, specialising in jams, peeled tomatoes and dried minestrone, and over time employed thousands of local women and men. The factory was also an important centre of local anti-Fascism. One of its workers was Werther Ricchi, who was already active in opposition to the regime before 25 July 1943; after 8 September he got jam and other products to the partisans of the 8th Garibaldi Brigade. He was arrested on 23 March 1944 and tortured. As the front moved through the area, the workforce helped save a large part of the factory’s machinery.",
  "poi_fantaguzzi_name": "Palazzo Fantaguzzi",
  "poi_fantaguzzi_desc": "Palazzo Fantaguzzi stands on today’s Corso Gastone Sozzi, formerly Corso Umberto I, and has housed the Banca Popolare di Cesena since 1922. During the first months of the Italian Social Republic, part of the building also housed the local Fascist offices; the sources do not show that it served as the Fascist headquarters throughout the entire dictatorship. Because of the symbolic importance of that presence, the Cesena GAP planned an attack. On 18 January 1944, four partisans set off a bomb at the entrance. There were no casualties, but the building was seriously damaged. A few weeks later, the local Fascist headquarters moved to Palazzo del Ridotto. The distinction matters: the sources place the later detention and torture of prisoners at the Ridotto, not at Palazzo Fantaguzzi.",
  "poi_rifugi_antiaerei_name": "Air-Raid Shelters of the Fortress",
  "poi_rifugi_antiaerei_desc": "The Viale Mazzoni shelter was a public tunnel excavated into the Rocca hill in 1944, designed by Mario Tellerini, the municipal chief engineer. It could accommodate up to 800 people, with 290 seats. It had two entrances protected by blast walls and included an infirmary, rooms for firefighters and the shelter supervisor, toilets, lighting, water and a telephone; the Germans later removed the telephone. The ventilation shaft ended in the Parco della Rimembranza and could also be used as an emergency exit. An extension to increase capacity to 1,500 people was considered but never built: the first major air raid on Cesena, on 13 May 1944, began a long period of bombing. The gallery was separate from the Malatestiana Fortress itself.",
